1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:02,980
In the previous episode, we discussed the working cycle of the Cloud Agent SDK.

2
00:00:02,980 --> 00:00:07,360
Today we continue reading Building Agents with the Cloud Agent SDK

3
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:09,280
the first step into the agent's loop

4
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:12,200
how does the agent collect the context required for tasks

5
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:14,960
In this chapter, Cloud discusses four strategies

6
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:15,800
divided into two categories

7
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:17,320
The first category is the collection strategy

8
00:00:17,320 --> 00:00:19,440
addresses how to acquire external information

9
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:22,790
including Agretic search and semantic search

10
00:00:22,790 --> 00:00:24,230
The second category is maintenance strategies

11
00:00:24,230 --> 00:00:26,610
addresses how to manage context capacity

12
00:00:26,610 --> 00:00:28,440
including sub-agents and compression

13
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:29,760
Why are they divided into these two categories

14
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:32,220
because the agent faces two fundamental challenges

15
00:00:32,220 --> 00:00:33,840
The first challenge is information retrieval

16
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:36,970
agents need to retrieve relevant information from massive data

17
00:00:36,970 --> 00:00:38,810
This is what the collection strategy aims to address

18
00:00:38,810 --> 00:00:40,690
The second challenge is capacity limitation

19
00:00:40,690 --> 00:00:42,650
The context window of large models is also limited.

20
00:00:42,650 --> 00:00:45,760
When the Agent runs for a long time or processes a large amount of information

21
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:47,000
the context window will overflow

22
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:48,660
This is what the maintenance strategy aims to address.

23
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:50,320
These two types of strategies are used in combination

24
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:52,800
so that the agent can find information while

25
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:54,340
the context window won't explode

26
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:55,900
Let's start with the two collection strategies

27
00:00:55,900 --> 00:00:56,440
The first type

28
00:00:56,440 --> 00:00:57,460
Aggentic search

29
00:00:57,460 --> 00:01:01,570
The file system represents information that can be pulled into the lower layer of the model cabin.

30
00:01:01,570 --> 00:01:03,590
when CD encounters large files

31
00:01:03,590 --> 00:01:05,610
such as logs or files uploaded by users

32
00:01:05,610 --> 00:01:06,850
it uses bash scripts

33
00:01:06,850 --> 00:01:10,310
such as grep and tail to determine how to load this content

34
00:01:10,310 --> 00:01:11,210
into the context

35
00:01:11,210 --> 00:01:13,130
First, let me explain what grep and tail are

36
00:01:13,130 --> 00:01:15,540
grep is a commonly used text search tool

37
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:19,160
Its function is to search for lines containing specific keywords in a file.

38
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:21,580
For example, if you have a log file with 100,000 lines

39
00:01:21,580 --> 00:01:23,860
You want to find all lines containing ERROR

40
00:01:23,860 --> 00:01:26,720
grep error log dot t x t

41
00:01:26,720 --> 00:01:29,610
It will print out all lines containing 'error'.

42
00:01:29,610 --> 00:01:31,430
tail is another commonly used tool

43
00:01:31,430 --> 00:01:34,010
Its function is to read the last few lines of a file

44
00:01:34,010 --> 00:01:36,229
For example, if you want to view the last 100 lines of a log file

45
00:01:36,229 --> 00:01:39,189
use tail -N100 log.txt

46
00:01:39,189 --> 00:01:41,750
This is particularly useful when checking the latest logs.

47
00:01:41,750 --> 00:01:43,690
Why using grep and tail is effective

48
00:01:43,690 --> 00:01:45,949
The first reason: precise filtering

49
00:01:45,949 --> 00:01:48,469
Suppose you have a 100MB log file

50
00:01:48,469 --> 00:01:51,229
If you directly dump the entire file into the Agent's context

51
00:01:51,229 --> 00:01:52,780
the upload window would crash

52
00:01:52,780 --> 00:01:55,500
but if you use grep to filter out lines containing 'error' first

53
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:56,800
probably only 100 lines

54
00:01:56,800 --> 00:01:58,120
only occupies 10KB

55
00:01:58,120 --> 00:02:00,230
The upper and lower door windows in Town A are sufficient.

56
00:02:00,230 --> 00:02:02,090
The second reason is traceable

57
00:02:02,090 --> 00:02:03,310
When the agent tells you

58
00:02:03,310 --> 00:02:04,550
he found an error

59
00:02:04,550 --> 00:02:06,070
You can see the commands it executed

60
00:02:06,070 --> 00:02:08,410
Grab erroog dot xt

61
00:02:08,410 --> 00:02:09,990
Do you know how he found it?

62
00:02:09,990 --> 00:02:11,610
You can verify whether the result is correct

63
00:02:11,610 --> 00:02:14,290
Transparency is slightly compromised when using vector search.

64
00:02:14,290 --> 00:02:16,330
The third reason is combinability

65
00:02:16,330 --> 00:02:19,750
grep and tail can be combined with other batch commands

66
00:02:19,750 --> 00:02:22,390
for example, grab error log dot t x t

67
00:02:22,390 --> 00:02:23,540
Tail -n 10

68
00:02:23,540 --> 00:02:24,980
first find all error lines

69
00:02:24,980 --> 00:02:26,240
then take the last ten lines

70
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:29,459
or grab e five ever log dot t x t

71
00:02:29,459 --> 00:02:30,859
After finding the error line

72
00:02:30,859 --> 00:02:33,180
then display the following five lines of context

73
00:02:33,180 --> 00:02:36,120
This combination of capabilities allows Agent to accomplish very complex information extraction tasks with minimal tools

74
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:38,380
accomplish highly complex information extraction tasks

75
00:02:38,380 --> 00:02:41,100
Next, let's look at the second context collection strategy

76
00:02:41,100 --> 00:02:43,140
semantic search

77
00:02:43,140 --> 00:02:46,079
It works by chunking the relevant context

78
00:02:46,079 --> 00:02:47,959
embed these chunks into vectors

79
00:02:47,959 --> 00:02:51,179
and then searches for concepts by querying these vectors

80
00:02:51,179 --> 00:02:53,510
To understand the difference between the two with an analogy

81
00:02:53,510 --> 00:02:56,030
Semantic search is like going to the archives to look up files

82
00:02:56,030 --> 00:02:58,570
you search by year and department with precision

83
00:02:58,570 --> 00:03:00,010
open the complete case file

84
00:03:00,010 --> 00:03:01,519
transparent and precise

85
00:03:01,519 --> 00:03:04,359
semantic search is like dissecting the contents of the file

86
00:03:04,359 --> 00:03:06,690
cut into individual paragraph segments

87
00:03:06,690 --> 00:03:08,330
and then categorizes them by meaning

88
00:03:08,330 --> 00:03:10,530
when you ask about the financial minting system

89
00:03:10,530 --> 00:03:12,830
will quickly pick out all the relevant segments

90
00:03:12,830 --> 00:03:14,050
quickly aggregated for you

91
00:03:14,050 --> 00:03:15,269
but with low accuracy

92
00:03:15,269 --> 00:03:16,229
difficult to maintain

93
00:03:16,229 --> 00:03:18,249
So when should each search method be used?

94
00:03:18,249 --> 00:03:20,579
cloud's suggestion in this article is

95
00:03:20,579 --> 00:03:23,399
Semantic Search is usually faster than agretic search

96
00:03:23,399 --> 00:03:24,419
but less accurate

97
00:03:24,419 --> 00:03:25,339
high maintenance difficulty

98
00:03:25,339 --> 00:03:26,540
transparency is also lower

99
00:03:26,540 --> 00:03:28,420
Given the limitations of semantic search

100
00:03:28,420 --> 00:03:31,760
It is recommended for agent scenarios that require exploring contextual structures

101
00:03:31,760 --> 00:03:33,730
should start with ijtic search

102
00:03:33,730 --> 00:03:37,330
only when faster results or more expressive variations are needed

103
00:03:37,330 --> 00:03:38,870
then introduce semantic search

104
00:03:38,870 --> 00:03:40,370
Here are two scenarios

105
00:03:40,370 --> 00:03:42,810
First, when faster search results are needed

106
00:03:42,810 --> 00:03:44,810
when IJTC search cannot meet the requirements

107
00:03:44,810 --> 00:03:46,370
Semantic search can be introduced

108
00:03:46,370 --> 00:03:49,340
Secondly, when user queries have many variations

109
00:03:49,340 --> 00:03:51,140
For example, users might use terms like 'error'

110
00:03:51,140 --> 00:03:53,959
exception failures to describe the same concept

111
00:03:53,959 --> 00:03:55,829
Semantic search can match all of these.

112
00:03:55,829 --> 00:03:58,329
while grep can only match keywords exactly

113
00:03:58,329 --> 00:04:00,949
This is what the original text refers to as more expressive variants

114
00:04:00,949 --> 00:04:03,740
The first two strategies address how to acquire information

115
00:04:03,740 --> 00:04:05,300
Now let's talk about two maintenance strategies

116
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:07,680
addresses how to manage context capacity

117
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:08,660
The first maintenance strategy

118
00:04:08,660 --> 00:04:10,940
Subligence cloud agent sdk

119
00:04:10,940 --> 00:04:12,810
supports sub-agents by default

120
00:04:12,810 --> 00:04:14,950
Sub-agents have two main purposes

121
00:04:14,950 --> 00:04:16,190
The first is parallelization

122
00:04:17,589 --> 00:04:18,750
Parallelization is easy to understand

123
00:04:18,750 --> 00:04:22,000
You can launch multiple sub-agents to handle different tasks simultaneously

124
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:23,360
What does context isolation mean?

125
00:04:23,360 --> 00:04:25,900
Sub-agents use their own independent context windows

126
00:04:25,900 --> 00:04:27,240
can handle large amounts of information

127
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:29,740
but only returns the relevant portions to the main agent

128
00:04:29,740 --> 00:04:30,650
For example

129
00:04:30,650 --> 00:04:32,230
When designing an email agent

130
00:04:32,230 --> 00:04:35,070
we may give it a search sub-agent capability

131
00:04:35,070 --> 00:04:38,690
The email agent can launch multiple search sub-agents in parallel

132
00:04:38,690 --> 00:04:41,640
each performs a different query on your email history

133
00:04:41,640 --> 00:04:43,500
and have them return only relevant excerpts

134
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:45,220
rather than the entire email thread

135
00:04:45,220 --> 00:04:48,000
This approach prevents the main agent's context from exploding

136
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:49,160
The second maintenance strategy

137
00:04:49,160 --> 00:04:50,650
compaction

138
00:04:50,650 --> 00:04:53,510
SUBVERANCE addresses the granularity in the spatial dimension

139
00:04:53,510 --> 00:04:56,030
Isolation is used to prevent the main agent from being overwhelmed

140
00:04:58,840 --> 00:05:01,260
Through compression, agents are enabled to run for extended periods

141
00:05:01,260 --> 00:05:05,880
The compression feature of Cloud Agent SDK activates when approaching context limits

142
00:05:05,880 --> 00:05:07,500
automatically summarize previous messages

143
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:09,500
so the agent won't run out of context

144
00:05:09,500 --> 00:05:12,700
This is built based on the Cloud Code compression slash command.

145
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:14,110
To summarize briefly

146
00:05:14,110 --> 00:05:16,510
There are four strategies for gathering context

147
00:05:16,510 --> 00:05:18,410
There are two information gathering strategies

148
00:05:18,410 --> 00:05:20,050
First, Agretic Search

149
00:05:20,050 --> 00:05:23,670
Searching the file system with grep and tail is transparent and accurate

150
00:05:23,670 --> 00:05:27,650
Second, semantic search with vector retrieval is fast but lacks transparency

151
00:05:27,650 --> 00:05:28,590
low accuracy

152
00:05:28,590 --> 00:05:29,679
high maintenance difficulty

153
00:05:29,679 --> 00:05:31,259
Use only when necessary

154
00:05:36,910 --> 00:05:39,490
prevent the main agent from being overwhelmed by excessive information

155
00:05:39,490 --> 00:05:42,700
Second, compaction automatically summarizes history

156
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:44,600
enable the agent to run for extended periods

157
00:05:44,600 --> 00:05:47,999
The core design philosophy is the file system and context framework

158
00:05:47,999 --> 00:05:50,439
let the agent decide where to find information

159
00:05:50,439 --> 00:05:53,260
Next episode we will delve into the 'take action' step.

160
00:05:53,260 --> 00:05:54,940
How exactly does the Agent execute tasks?

161
00:05:54,940 --> 00:05:56,780
what tools does cloud provide

162
